{
  "image_name": "f2930050766441001e329fb551dba9cb.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how thermokarst lakes and permafrost landscapes evolve from the end of the Last Glacial Maximum through the Holocene and into a warmer future, contrasting ice-rich and ice-poor permafrost settings. In ice-rich terrain, the landscape begins with abundant ice wedges forming polygonal ground and only limited lake formation. As climate warms during deglaciation, thaw of ground ice drives widespread thermokarst, creating many lakes and subsurface thaw zones beneath them. Later, in the mid to late Holocene, some lakes drain and secondary thermokarst lakes form, while parts of the ground appear to refreeze or stabilize. Under future warming, the figure suggests continued permafrost degradation, more lake development, and greater drainage, alongside reduced permafrost re-aggradation.\n\nIn ice-poor permafrost, the starting surface lacks polygonal ice-wedge networks and instead contains pre-existing depressions. Lake initiation becomes more widespread during early warming as permafrost degrades, but later stages emphasize lake drainage and renewed permafrost aggradation. In the warmer-future panel, secondary thermokarst lakes still develop, though the overall response appears more subdued than in ice-rich terrain. Overall, the figure highlights that ground-ice content strongly controls lake formation, drainage, and long-term permafrost change.",
